---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Why Blox held an order instead of cancelling it';
const description =
	'When Blox has already cancelled several orders for the same customer, it holds the next one for a Manual Blox Review.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} noindex>
	<article class="doc section">
		<div class="site-wrap doc__wrap">
			<span class="section-label">Docs</span>
			<h1>{title}</h1>
			<div class="doc__body">
				<p>
					Blox had already cancelled several orders for this customer in a short time. That’s
					unusual, so it held this order for a Manual Blox Review rather than cancelling and
					refunding another one.
				</p>
				<p>
					A run of cancellations sometimes means a legitimate customer was flagged, or that more
					than one shopper ended up on the same profile. Cancelling and refunding can’t be undone,
					so Blox holds the order and a member of the Blox team reviews that customer’s recent
					orders.
				</p>

				<h2>How this differs from a normal Blox hold</h2>
				<p>
					On a normal Blox hold, you still need to cancel the order or release the hold. This one
					is different: Blox is reviewing the recent orders, and the notification won’t ask you to
					cancel or release it. You can still open the customer from the link in your notification.
				</p>
				<p>This applies to one customer, not your whole store.</p>
				<p>
					Questions? Email <a href="mailto:hello@heyblox.com">hello@heyblox.com</a>.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.doc {
		padding-top: 3.5rem;
		padding-bottom: 5rem;
	}

	.doc__wrap {
		max-width: 46rem;
	}

	.doc h1 {
		font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 2.75rem);
		margin-bottom: 2.25rem;
	}

	.doc__body h2 {
		margin: 2.5rem 0 0.85rem;
		font-size: 1.35rem;
	}

	.doc__body p {
		margin: 0.85rem 0;
		color: var(--color-fg-muted);
	}

	.doc__body a {
		color: var(--color-fg);
	}

	.doc__body strong {
		color: var(--color-fg);
		font-weight: 600;
	}
</style>
